WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.580
Let's walk through knitting a Swan Sock.

2
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.140
The construction of this sock is unusual,

3
00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:11.860
but it's not hard if you focus
on each section one at a time.

4
00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:15.380
We'll start at the leg or ribbing

5
00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:23.400
and then knit the heel, sole and toe,
ending with the instep.

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.780
Because the sole and instep are knit
separately, the sock allows for a lot

7
00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.700
of creativity with color
and stitch patterning.

8
00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:33.740
For my sample,

9
00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:38.560
I've changed colors for each section
to help make things more clear.

10
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.460
Instead of ribbing,
I just cast on with waste yarn and ravel cord

11
00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:46.960
and knit a one inch hem
in the dark blue color.

12
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.260
After knitting the ankle in light blue,
I scrapped off half of the stitches.

13
00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.720
We'll pick these up again later and join

14
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.720
them to the instep
stitches to finish the sock.

15
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.940
Next in dark blue is the heel flap,
the original pattern.

16
00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.560
Suggest using a heel stitch or eye

17
00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:12.380
of partridge stitch for a bit of padding
and durability. With your first sock

18
00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:17.280
I highly suggest knitting
that heel flap plain for now.

19
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.620
Next, in light pink is the heel turn.

20
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:29.680
If you've knit a sock heel before, this
is just one half of a normal heel or toe.

21
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.780
Now the fun starts with knitting

22
00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:41.660
the gusset. In light blue I cast
on the wings of section five.

23
00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:47.480
To make this easier, I've used two small
yellow cast on rags and a row of ravel

24
00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.260
cord. With this in place,

25
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:57.930
I can double eWrap and knit the cast on
cleanly with weights on the new stitches.

26
00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:04.560
The light blue gusset section is shaped
with decreases to create those wings.

27
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.960
Next is the sole of the sock.

28
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.380
You can get creative in the center part
of the gusset and the sole section,

29
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:22.020
like this red and white version, adding
a bit of Fairisle with small floats.

30
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:27.500
But again, I suggest knitting a plain sock
first to get a feel for the overall

31
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:32.680
construction before adding
any stitch patterns.

32
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.890
My toe is dark blue.

33
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:42.100
Nothing fancy, just short row decreases
and increases to form the toe pocket.

34
00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:48.460
To finish the knitting, I switched
to dark pink and knit the instep.

35
00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:53.060
After scrapping off with orange
and using a little steam.

36
00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.920
How is this going to become a sock?

37
00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.780
First, the instep is joined to the ankle

38
00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:06.040
with kitchener grafting joining
the orange scrapped off edges.

39
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.900
The cast on wings are sewn to the heel flap

40
00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:16.960
and the instep, sole and gusset are
joined along the red dotted lines.

41
00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:24.260
This may not be the prettiest sock ever,
but it's a good exercise for following

42
00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.680
each one of the steps in this
unusually constructed sock.

